(1. DEFINITION OF SCALE & REPRESENTATIVE FRACTION \(RF\): Scale expresses the) Tj
T*
(fixed mathematical ratio between a distance measured on the map and the) Tj
T*
(corresponding distance on the ground. RF 1:50,000 means 1 unit on the map) Tj
T*
(equals 50,000 identical units on the terrain.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. RELIEF & CONTOUR LINES: Relief represents the three-dimensional) Tj
T*
(elevation variations of the earth's surface. Contours are brown lines) Tj
T*
(connecting points of equal height above Mean Sea Level \(MSL\). The vertical) Tj
T*
(distance between adjacent contour lines is termed the Vertical Interval) Tj
T*
(\(VI\), standardly 20 meters.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. TERRAIN FEATURE IDENTIFICATION: Contour lines positioned close together) Tj
T*
(indicate a steep slope or cliff face. Contour lines widely spaced signify a) Tj
T*
(gentle, flat plain. Concentric circular contours with height numbers) Tj
T*
(increasing toward the center indicate a Hill Peak, while concentric) Tj
T*
(contours with numbers decreasing inward denote a Crater or Basin.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4. SPURS, VALLEYS, AND SADDLES: A V-shaped contour pointing down towards) Tj
T*
(lower elevation represents a Spur \(ridge\). A V-shaped contour pointing up) Tj
T*
(towards higher elevation represents a Valley or Re-entrant. A dip between) Tj
T*
(two adjacent peaks is a Saddle or Col.) Tj
